// ============================================================
// ONE DAY — Full Script (all chapters)
// ============================================================

state
  glukose: 8.4
  trend: rising
  insulinOnBoard: 0
  basalAktiv: false
  stressLevel: 10
  bewegungAktiv: false
  uhrzeit: "04:00"
  pfadMorgen: ""
  gerannt: false
  frueshtueckswahl: ""
  bolusTiming: ""
  oeffentlichSpritzen: ""
  kohlenhydratSchaetzung: ""
  abendessenWahl: ""
  persona: ""
  playerName: ""
  anfangsEinschaetzung: ""

character chef
  name: "Boss"

character robin
  name: "Robin"

// Balu = the voice of the body (blood glucose). Colour is FIXED (Aare blue/petrol),
// regardless of what he says. The host always prefixes him with a red
// droplet (🩸), independent of the mood system (that belongs to Lumi only).
character balu
  name: "Balu"
  color: "#1E4548"

// Lumi = the emotion/mood. Colour AND emoji change per line via the
// mood_* tag (see the blueprint document for the full colour table).
// The host always prefixes her with a star (⭐), coloured to match
// the current mood_* tag.
character lumi
  name: "Lumi"

// Interfaces to the React app / audio engine.
// A "function" declaration WITHOUT an indented body counts in Loreline
// as automatically external and is implemented by the host app.
// Important for the JS adapter: all arguments arrive as ONE array,
// e.g. playSound(interpreter, args) with args = ["glucose_alarm"].
function playSound(name, caption)
function playAmbience(name, loop, caption)
function applyEffect(type, magnitude, delaySeconds)
function setCurve(value, trend, zeit)
function logDecision(id)
function pauseBeat(seconds)
function revealDayCurve()
function offerShare()
function unlockKnowledge(id, title, text)

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// CHAPTER 0 — Welcome
// ------------------------------------------------------------

beat Open
  Are you ready for a change of perspective?
  choice
    I'm ready.
      -> Szenenwechsel

    No, rather not.
      Oh.
      pauseBeat(1)
      Alright.
      pauseBeat(2)
      ...
      pauseBeat(1)
      Doesn't it make you curious what it would be like to take over the functions of one of your organs?
      pauseBeat(1)
      choice
        Alright, if you put it that way!
          -> Szenenwechsel

        No, I'd rather stay in my comfort zone.
          Alright. See you next time, then.
          -> .

beat Szenenwechsel
  Great! Here we go.
  pauseBeat(1)
  -> Willkommen

beat Willkommen
  I'm Alex.
  pauseBeat(1)
  I've been living with Type 1 diabetes for a year.
  pauseBeat(1)
  Today, you are me.
  pauseBeat(1)
  I'm handing you the reins.
  pauseBeat(1)
  You'll be making decisions that shape the course of your blood glucose curve.
  pauseBeat(2)
  <disclaimer> This experience is an experimental learning format and not a substitute for medical advice or treatment.
  pauseBeat(2)
  choice
    Understood.
      -> DisclaimerBestaetigt

beat DisclaimerBestaetigt
  Before we start, Alex, one question...
  pauseBeat(2)
  What do you think — how well will you do?
  pauseBeat(1)
  -> Einschaetzung

beat Einschaetzung
  choice
    I'm confident I'll manage this well.
      anfangsEinschaetzung := zuversichtlich
      -> EinschaetzungBestaetigt

    Hmm, I don't know...
      anfangsEinschaetzung := unsicher
      -> EinschaetzungBestaetigt

    That sounds complicated, I don't think so well.
      anfangsEinschaetzung := kompliziert
      -> EinschaetzungBestaetigt

    - <first_knowledge>❓ What is diabetes??
        unlockKnowledge("diabetes", "What is diabetes?", "Diabetes means the body can no longer regulate blood glucose on its own. In Type 1, the pancreas no longer produces insulin, so it has to be supplied from outside several times a day. It used to be called juvenile diabetes, since the autoimmune condition is usually diagnosed in childhood or adolescence. Type 2 usually develops gradually in adulthood and is classified as metabolic disease. It is partly genetic. Insulin is present, but the cells barely respond to it anymore. In many cases it can be positively influenced through lifestyle changes and other medications, so insulin isn't always needed.")

        Diabetes means the body can no longer regulate blood glucose on its own.
        pauseBeat(2)
        In Type 1, the pancreas no longer produces insulin, so it has to be supplied from outside several times a day.
        pauseBeat(2)
        There are different ways to deliver insulin, but it is usually injected.
        pauseBeat(2)
        People living with Type 1 diabetes usually have 2 options: 
        pauseBeat(1)
        Inject manually with an insulin pen or syringe, or use an insulin pump that automates part of the insulin delivery.
        pauseBeat(3)
        The choice depends on the person, technological availability and insurance policies of each country.
        pauseBeat(3)
        If you'd like to know more, you can tap 💡 in the status bar.
        Over the course of today, every "aha" moment lands in your status bar. You can revisit them any time.
        pauseBeat(3)
        So, what do you think — how well will you do?
        -> Einschaetzung

beat EinschaetzungBestaetigt
  Keep your answer in mind. We'll come back to it at the end.
  pauseBeat(1)
  Which of these fits you best?
  choice
    I live with Type 1 or insulin-dependent diabetes.
      persona := typ1
      -> WillkommenTyp1

    I live with diabetes but don't take insulin.
      persona := diabetesOhneInsulin
      -> WillkommenOhneInsulin

    I don't live with diabetes.
      persona := keinDiabetes
      -> WillkommenKeinDiabetes

beat WillkommenTyp1
  You're one of roughly 9 million people worldwide living with Type 1 diabetes.
  pauseBeat(2)
  Together, that's about the population of Switzerland! (And rising, as of 2025.)
  pauseBeat(1)
  Glad you're here.
  pauseBeat(1)
  Some of these situations will probably feel familiar.
  pauseBeat(1)
  The goal of this little experience is to give people without diabetes a glimpse into our everyday life.
  pauseBeat(1)
  That blood glucose isn't just a number, but a living curve that's constantly changing.
  pauseBeat(2)
  It's hard to explain what it feels like to live with diabetes.
  pauseBeat(1)
  Everyone's daily life looks different. Every day brings new surprises.
  pauseBeat(1)
  But maybe you'll recognise yourself in some of these situations.
  pauseBeat(1)
  Shall we start?
  choice
    Let's go!
      -> Alarm

beat WillkommenOhneInsulin
  Great that you're here!
  pauseBeat(1)
  Since you live with diabetes yourself, you know the effects a chronic condition can have on everyday life.
  pauseBeat(1)
  These effects aren't always visible, and sometimes not even obvious to yourself.
  pauseBeat(1)
  But they're there.
  pauseBeat(1)
  In a moment, you'll live a day with Type 1 diabetes and experience how it changes everyday life.
  pauseBeat(1)
  Ready?
  choice
    Let's go!
      -> Alarm

beat WillkommenKeinDiabetes
  Thanks for your curiosity!
  pauseBeat(2)
  In a moment, you'll live a day with Type 1 diabetes and experience how it changes everyday life.
  pauseBeat(1)
  You might recognise this from other chronic conditions too.
  pauseBeat(1)
  There's one more invisible layer involved.
  pauseBeat(1)
  That's hard to explain...
  pauseBeat(2)
  Best if I just show you. Ready?
  choice
    Let's go!
      -> Alarm


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// CHAPTER 1 — Night, 4am
// ------------------------------------------------------------

beat Alarm
  playAmbience("bedroom_silence", true, "Silence in the bedroom")
  playSound("glucose_alarm", "Glucose alarm sounds")

  A shrill alarm tears you out of sleep.

  pauseBeat(1)
  It's dark. You blink at your phone.
  pauseBeat(1)

  $uhrzeit.

  setCurve(glukose, trend, uhrzeit)

  At the top of the screen you see your glucose reading.
  It's slightly above the target range for nighttime.
  pauseBeat(2)

  balu: $glukose mmol/l. Rising.

  pauseBeat(2)

  lumi: <mood_muede> Oh no... not again.

  pauseBeat(2)
  You're tired. Very tired.
  Your phone nearly blinds you.
  pauseBeat(2)
  Part of you just wants to keep sleeping.
  But the high reading is still there.
  pauseBeat(2)
  What do you do?
  pauseBeat(1)
  -> KorrekturEntscheidung


beat KorrekturEntscheidung
  choice
    I'll take the correction dose. This reading is too high for me.
      logDecision("kap1_korrigiert")
      pfadMorgen := A
      -> KorrekturJa

    I'll ignore the alarm and keep sleeping for now. You'll correct once you reach 9mmol.
      logDecision("kap1_ignoriert")
      pfadMorgen := B
      -> KorrekturNein

    ❓ What counts as "in range"?
      -> ZielbereichWissen


beat ZielbereichWissen
  unlockKnowledge("zielbereich", "What counts as \"in range\"?", "For many people with Type 1 diabetes, roughly 3.9 to 10 mmol/l counts as being \"in range.\" Above that is called hyperglycemia, below it hypoglycemia. The exact target is individual and is usually discussed and set together with your diabetologist.")

  <knowledge>
  For many people with Type 1 diabetes, roughly 3.9 to 10 mmol/l counts as being "in range." Above that is called hyperglycemia, below it hypoglycemia. The exact target is individual and is usually discussed and set together with your diabetologist.

  pauseBeat(2)

  -> KorrekturEntscheidungNochmal


beat KorrekturEntscheidungNochmal
  choice
    I'll take the correction dose. This reading is too high for me.
      logDecision("kap1_korrigiert")
      pfadMorgen := A
      -> KorrekturJa

    I'll ignore the alarm and keep sleeping for now.
      logDecision("kap1_ignoriert")
      pfadMorgen := B
      -> KorrekturNein

beat KorrekturJa
  playSound("pen_open", "Insulin pen opens")
  playSound("inject", "Injection")

  You give yourself a small correction dose.

  pauseBeat(1)

  insulinOnBoard += 0.5
  applyEffect("bolus_correction", 1.5, 900)

  -> KorrekturJaWissen


beat KorrekturJaWissen
  choice
    - ❓ Learn more
        unlockKnowledge("spritzstellen", "Injection sites", "Common injection sites include the belly and the upper arms. Rotating between sites helps prevent lumps forming under the skin from repeated injections in the same spot, which can affect how well insulin is absorbed.")

        <knowledge>
        Common injection sites include the belly and the upper arms. Rotating between sites helps prevent lumps forming under the skin from repeated injections in the same spot, which can affect how well insulin is absorbed.

        pauseBeat(2)

        -> KorrekturJaWissen

    NEXT
      -> KorrekturJaWeiter


beat KorrekturJaWeiter
  The alarm goes quiet. You roll over again.
  Slowly, the room falls silent again.

  playAmbience("bedroom_silence", true, "Silence in the bedroom")

  pauseBeat(1)
  Then you drift back to sleep.
  pauseBeat(1)
  ...zzzZZZzzz...
  pauseBeat(3)
  -> Morgen


beat KorrekturNein
  Not now.
  pauseBeat(2)
  If you hit 9 mmol you'll correct then.
  pauseBeat(2)
  You put the phone back down.

  playSound("alarm_dismiss", "Alarm is silenced")

  applyEffect("dawn_phenomenon", 2, 0)
  pauseBeat(2)

  <tag_sleepy> The alarm goes quiet. You roll over again.
  Slowly, the room falls silent again and you drift back to sleep.

  playAmbience("bedroom_silence", true, "Silence in the bedroom")

  pauseBeat(2)
  ...zzzZZZzzz...
  pauseBeat(3)
  -> Morgen


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// CHAPTER 2 — Morning, 6:30am
// ------------------------------------------------------------

beat Morgen
  uhrzeit := "06:30"
  playSound("alarm_clock", "Alarm clock rings")
  playAmbience("birds_outside", true, "Birds chirping outside")

  The alarm clock rings.
  pauseBeat(2)
  $uhrzeit.
  pauseBeat(2)

  if pfadMorgen == "A"
    -> MorgenPfadA
  else
    -> MorgenPfadB


beat MorgenPfadA
  You slowly open your eyes.
  pauseBeat(2)
  The first thing you reach for, automatically, is your basal insulin.

  -> Basalinsulin


beat MorgenPfadB
  You feel heavy. Your mouth is dry. You're thirsty.
  pauseBeat(2)
  The first thing you reach for, automatically, is your basal insulin.

  -> Basalinsulin


beat Basalinsulin
  playSound("pen_open", "Insulin pen opens")
  playSound("inject", "Injection")

  pauseBeat(2)
  You give yourself your daily dose. It's part of the routine.

  basalAktiv = true
  insulinOnBoard += 1
  applyEffect("basal", 1, 4800)

  pendingKnowledgeId = "insulin_arten"
  pendingKnowledgeTitle = "Basal insulin vs. bolus insulin"
  pendingKnowledgeText = "There are different types of insulin. Basal insulin acts slowly over many hours and covers the body's baseline need. Bolus insulin acts fast and is used for meals or to correct high readings. If you inject with a pen, you use two different types of insulin: a slow and a fast one. The insulin pump only uses a fast acting insulin to deliver a basal rate, thhat is given in tiny amounts every couple of minutes throughout the day."

  -> BasalinsulinWissen


beat BasalinsulinWissen
  choice
    - ❓ Learn more
        <knowledge> $pendingKnowledgeText
        unlockKnowledge(pendingKnowledgeId, pendingKnowledgeTitle, pendingKnowledgeText)
        -> BasalinsulinWissen

    NEXT
      -> BasalinsulinWeiter


beat BasalinsulinWeiter

  Then you put the pen back on the nightstand.
  pauseBeat(2)

  if pfadMorgen == "A"
    Now you check your phone.

    setCurve(6.4, "stable", uhrzeit)

    pauseBeat(2)
    Your value looks stable.
    pauseBeat(1)
    You feel surprisingly rested. A good start.
    pauseBeat(2)

    balu: I'm stable.
  else
    Before you're even properly awake, you reach for your phone.

    setCurve(10, "rising", uhrzeit)

    pauseBeat(2)
    You wince slightly.
    pauseBeat(1)
    balu: 10 mmol/l. Rising.
    pauseBeat(2)
    You already know the culprit:
    pauseBeat(2)
    That big portion of pasta from last night.
    pauseBeat(1)
    And your usual morning rise.
    pauseBeat(2)

    lumi: <mood_frust> This isn't how I wanted to start the day...

    -> MorgenResistenzWissen

  -> Badezimmer


beat MorgenResistenzWissen
  choice
    - ❓ Learn more
        unlockKnowledge("morgen_resistenz", "Why mornings are different", "Insulin needs are often higher in the morning than later in the day. Hormones released just before waking make many people temporarily more insulin-resistant and can raise blood sugar levels without having eaten. Bad sleep can make this effect even stronger.")

        <knowledge>
        Insulin needs are often higher in the morning than later in the day.
        Hormones released just before waking make many people temporarily more insulin-resistant and can raise blood sugar levels without having eaten anything.

        pauseBeat(1)

        -> MorgenResistenzWissen

    NEXT
      -> Badezimmer


beat Badezimmer
  playSound("bathroom_door", "Bathroom door opens")
  playSound("faucet_water", "Swoosh — water running in the shower")

  You head to the shower.

  if pfadMorgen == "A"
    pauseBeat(2)
    You step under the water. The earlier reading is already fine.

    setCurve(5.4, "falling", uhrzeit)
    pauseBeat(1)
    ....swooooosh....
    pauseBeat(3)
    You step out of the shower and pat yourself dry. 
    pauseBeat(2)
    Then you put on the clothes you prepared yesterday evening.
    pauseBeat(2)
    Time for breakfast!
  else
    pauseBeat(1)
    First, though, you give yourself a small correction dose into your belly, then step under the water.
    pauseBeat(2)
    Who knows, maybe the insulin works faster under warm water. At least that's something you read in a pamphlet some time ago.

    setCurve(7.2, "falling", uhrzeit)

    -> BadezimmerKorrekturWissen

  -> Fruehstueck


beat BadezimmerKorrekturWissen
  choice
    - ❓ Learn more
        unlockKnowledge("spritzstellen", "Injection sites", "Common injection sites for bolus insulin include the belly and the upper arms. Rotating between sites helps prevent lumps forming under the skin from repeated injections in the same spot, which can affect how well insulin is absorbed.")

        <knowledge>
        Common injection sites include the belly and the upper arms. Rotating between sites helps prevent lumps forming under the skin from repeated injections in the same spot, which can affect how well insulin is absorbed.

        pauseBeat(2)

        -> BadezimmerKorrekturWissen

    NEXT
      -> BadezimmerKorrekturWeiter


beat BadezimmerKorrekturWeiter
  pauseBeat(1)
  ....swooooosh....
  pauseBeat(3)
  You step out of the shower and pat yourself dry.
  pauseBeat(2)
  Then you put on the clothes you prepared yesterday evening.
  pauseBeat(2)
  Time for breakfast!

  -> Fruehstueck


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// CHAPTER 2 (cont.) — Breakfast & Time Pressure
// ------------------------------------------------------------

beat Fruehstueck
  playSound("fridge_open", "Fridge opens")
  pauseBeat(2)

  You open the fridge. Today you have two options.

  choice
    Muesli with berries
      frueshtueckswahl := muesli
      -> Zeitdruck

    Scrambled eggs with wholegrain bread
      frueshtueckswahl := ruehrei
      -> Zeitdruck

    ❓ What else affects the curve?
      -> FruehstueckWissen


beat FruehstueckWissen
  unlockKnowledge("naehrstoffe_kurve", "What else affects the curve", "It's not just sugar or carbohydrates that affect the glucose curve. Protein and fat influence it too, usually more slowly and over a longer period than carbohydrates alone.")

  <knowledge>
  It's not just sugar or carbohydrates that affect the glucose curve. Protein and fat influence it too, usually more slowly and over a longer period than carbohydrates alone. So different food combinations need different insulin strategies.

  pauseBeat(1)

  -> FruehstueckNochmal


beat FruehstueckNochmal
  choice
    Muesli with berries
      frueshtueckswahl := muesli
      -> Zeitdruck

    Scrambled eggs with wholegrain bread
      frueshtueckswahl := ruehrei
      -> Zeitdruck

beat Zeitdruck
  Suddenly your eyes land on the clock.
  pauseBeat(1)

  uhrzeit := "07:18"
  $uhrzeit. The train leaves at 07:39. You can make it if you hurry up and run.
  pauseBeat(1)

  if pfadMorgen == "A"
    setCurve(5.4, "stable", uhrzeit)
  else
    setCurve(7.2, "stable", uhrzeit)

  How did time get by so quickly?!
  pauseBeat(1)
  What do you do?
  pauseBeat(1)

  choice
    Run. You just started this job 3 months ago. You can't afford to be late!
      pauseBeat(1)
      gerannt = true
      logDecision("kap2_rennen")
      -> Bahnhof

    Catch the next train. There's nothing you hate more than rushing your mornings.
      gerannt = false
      logDecision("kap2_gemuetlich")
      -> Bahnhof


beat Bahnhof
  playSound("front_door", "Front door shuts as you leave for the station")

  if gerannt
    -> BahnhofGerannt
  else
    -> BahnhofGemuetlich


beat BahnhofGerannt
  You inject quickly, weigh your usual portion and prepare the food.
  pauseBeat(1)
  1, 2 3 bites. You're done. Go!
  insulinOnBoard += 3
  applyEffect("bolus_meal", 3, 900)

  playSound("footsteps_running", "Running footsteps and heavy breathing")
  playSound("train_doors_close", "Train doors close")
  bewegungAktiv = true
  applyEffect("bewegung_rennen", 2.5, 1200)
  pauseBeat(2)
  ....runRUNrun....
  pauseBeat(3)

  The doors close. Made it.
  pauseBeat(2)
  You take a deep breath. You sit down.

  Slowly, your gaze drifts upward. Your blood glucose level is changing.

  if frueshtueckswahl == "muesli"
    setCurve(9.8, "rising", uhrzeit)
    First it rises. Then it suddenly drops fast.
    setCurve(6.2, "falling", uhrzeit)
  else if frueshtueckswahl == "ruehrei"
    setCurve(7.8, "rising", uhrzeit)
    Only a small rise. Then it starts to fall.
    setCurve(6.0, "falling", uhrzeit)

  balu: Something is happening here...
  pauseBeat(2)
  lumi: <mood_sorge> Oh no why are we dropping? 
  pauseBeat(2)
  lumi: <mood_sorge> Did I run to fast
  pauseBeat(1)
  lumi: <mood_sorge> Or is the insulin kicking in now?
  pauseBeat(1)
  What do you think?

  -> BewegungInterpretation

beat BewegungInterpretation
  choice
    I think it was the running.
      logDecision("kap2_deutung_rennen")
      You guess the activity may be the culprit and keep an eye on your status bar.
      pauseBeat(2)
      You'll soon arrive at the office anyway. 
      .....rock-rock....
      pauseBeat(3)
      -> BewegungWissen

    For sure the insulin I took.
      logDecision("kap2_deutung_insulin")
      Maybe the insulin you just took before leaving the house is starting to work now.
      pauseBeat(2)
      You just keep an eye on the status bar. You'll be at the office soon enough anyway.
      .....rock-rock....
      pauseBeat(3)
      -> BewegungWissen

    Probably both of them together.
      logDecision("kap2_deutung_beides")
      Probably tje combination of both: a lot of active insulin on board from your breakfast, and then you running to the station.
      pauseBeat(2)
      Maybe it was too much at once. You jsut keep an eye on the Status bar for now. 
      pauseBeat(2)
      You'll soon be at the office anyway.
      pauseBeat(2)
      .....rock-rock....
      pauseBeat(3)
      -> BewegungWissen
      
beat BewegungWissen
  choice
    - ❓ Learn more
        unlockKnowledge("bewegung_aktivitaet", "How movement affects it", "Activity affects blood glucose. Depending on the situation, it can cause a quick rise or a quick drop. Cardio tends to make it drop, whereas explosive movements or strength training can momentarily raise it. The drop in blood glucose level is even stronger, if there is a lot of insulin in your system. People with Type 1 diabetes can still be phyically active, they just have to develop strategies to especially prevent hypoglycemia. One safety measure is to always carry some sugary snacks with you that can raise blood glucose quickly when needed.")

        <knowledge>
        You already know that there is more than food that affects blood glucose. One other factor is activity. Depending on the situation and activity, it
        can cause a quick rise or a quick drop in your blood sugar levels, especially, if you still have active insulin "on board". It is important to find out, how activity impacts your blood glucose and how long the insulin stays in your system.

        pauseBeat(3)

        -> BewegungWissen

    NEXT
      -> Arbeit


beat BahnhofGemuetlich
  Everything is fine, no no need to rush.
  pauseBeat(2)

  playSound("pen_open", "Insulin pen opens")
  playSound("inject", "Injection")

  insulinOnBoard += 3

  You inject your Bolus Insulin, weigh your usual protions, prepare your food.
  pauseBeat(2)
  ...miam....
  pauseBeat(3)
  applyEffect("bolus_meal", 3, 900)
  Then you put your shoes on, grab your bag and out you go.
  
  playSound("footsteps_calm", "Calm footsteps")
  pauseBeat(2)

  Out to the station.

  playSound("train_arrival", "Train arrives")

  pauseBeat(2)

  setCurve(6.6, "stable", uhrzeit)

  The next train arrives. The curve stays calm.
  pauseBeat(2)
  You board, relaxed.

  -> Arbeit


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// CHAPTER 3 — Work
// ------------------------------------------------------------

beat Arbeit
  playSound("office_door", "Office door opens as your boss walks in")
  playAmbience("office_ambience", true, "Office background noise")
  uhrzeit := "08:19"

  pauseBeat(2)
  You've barely sat down at your desk when the office door opens.

  if gerannt
    setCurve(6.3, "stable", uhrzeit)
  else
    setCurve(7, "stable", uhrzeit)

  if gerannt
    chef: Alex! Good, you're here. A client showed up unannounced and Sam is with another client. Can you take over the presentation at 8:30?
  else
    chef: Where were you? A client showed up unannounced. Sam is out with another client. Can you present your project in their stead at 8:30?

  lumi: <mood_gestresst> Not today, please.

  Your gaze drifts up to the clock. Ten minutes left. 
  pauseBeat(2)
  You nod and smile politely.
  pauseBeat(2)
  Time to shine.
  pauseBeat(2)

  Meanwhile, something happens. Very quietly.

  stressLevel += 40
  applyEffect("stress", 2, 300)

  if gerannt
    setCurve(10.8, "rising", uhrzeit)
  else
    setCurve(11.9, "rising", uhrzeit)

  And still it keeps rising.

  balu: Hm... I'm rising.
  pauseBeat(2)
  lumi: What's happening? Why are we rising??
  pauseBeat(2)

  What do you think is pushing the curve up right now?

  choice
    The stress from the presentation.
      logDecision("kap3_deutung_stress")

    Breakfast is only really kicking in now.
      logDecision("kap3_deutung_fruehstueck")

    The running from earlier, delayed. if gerannt
      logDecision("kap3_deutung_bewegung")

    No idea, honestly.
      logDecision("kap3_deutung_unsicher")

  -> StressWissen

beat StressWissen
  choice
    - ❓ Learn more
        unlockKnowledge("stress_blutzucker", "How stress affects it", "It's often not just one reason. Stress can raise blood glucose. Hormones like cortisol and adrenaline cause the liver to release glucose. That's a normal response of the body. ")

        <knowledge>
        It's often not just one reason. Stress can raise blood glucose. Hormones like cortisol and adrenaline cause the liver to release glucose. That's a normal response of the body, which the body usually regulates by secreting more insulin. People with Type 1 diabetes don't produce insulin anymore, therefore they need to respond themselves by injecting.

        pauseBeat(3)

        -> StressWissen

    NEXT
      -> ArbeitAbschluss


beat ArbeitAbschluss
  pauseBeat(1)
  Hmmm... You are probably stressed out.
  pauseBeat(2)
  Even though you have been working on this project from the beginning 3 months ago, Sam is the lead.
  pauseBeat(2)
  But you want to show what you got, so you get ready and put together a presentation.
  pauseBeat(2)
  It's showtime!
  pauseBeat(2)
  ....blaBLAbla....
  pauseBeat(3)

  uhrzeit := "10:02"
  if gerannt
    setCurve(10.8, "rising", uhrzeit)
  else
    setCurve(11.9, "rising", uhrzeit)

  The presentation is over. The client thanks you briefly.
  pauseBeat(2)
  Their face doesn't give much away.
  pauseBeat(2)

  "We'll be in touch." They don't say more. Then they're gone.
  pauseBeat(2)
  You don't know whether that was good or bad. But it's done!
  pauseBeat(2)
  Now it's time to finally continue working on your other project.
  pauseBeat(2)
  ....workwork....
  pauseBeat(3)

  -> Mittag


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// CHAPTER 4 — Lunch
// ------------------------------------------------------------

beat Mittag
  playSound("cafeteria_door", "Cafeteria door opens")
  playAmbience("cafeteria_ambience", true, "Voices and dishes in the cafeteria")
  uhrzeit := "11:55"

  The tension from the presentation is still sitting in your shoulders.
  pauseBeat(2)
  But you are starting to think about lunch.
  pauseBeat(2)
  $uhrzeit.
  pauseBeat(1)
  You hear the door opening and a familiar head poking through.
  pauseBeat(2)
  robin: Alex, we're heading over to the cantine. Are you coming with us?
  pauseBeat(2)
  Right on time.
  pauseBeat(2)

  Today it's the standard menu again. Lucky for you, you already know it and how tu Bolus for it.
  pauseBeat(2)
  But sometimes you feel weird when injecting in public.
  pauseBeat(2)
  You hesitate.
  pauseBeat(2)
  What do you want to do?

  applyEffect("kohlenhydrate_mahlzeit", 3, 900)

  choice
    Head to the cantine with Robin and inject at the table.
      oeffentlichSpritzen := tisch
      logDecision("kap4_tisch")
      playSound("pen_open", "Insulin pen opens")
      playSound("inject", "Injection")

      pauseBeat(2)
      You grab your bag and join them.
      pauseBeat(2)
      ....food being served....
      pauseBeat(2)
      At the table, you pull out your insulin pen, scroll on a new needle and lift your shirt just enough to see your lower belly.
      pauseBeat(2)
      Even though you tried to be discreet, you notice some gazes.
      pauseBeat(2)
      lumi: <mood_scham> I knew this was going to happen...
      pauseBeat(2)
      You've had some bad experiences when injecting in public. 
      pauseBeat(2)
      One time someone thought you were a junkie.
      pauseBeat(2)
      Another time you injected in a train, and the person next to you seemed... discusted.
      pauseBeat(2)
      Of course most of the times nothing happened and nobody cared.
      pauseBeat(2)
      But these experiences impacted you...
      pauseBeat(3)
      Robin looks at you, curious and a bit confused.
      pauseBeat(2)
      They see you injecting for the first time.
      pauseBeat(2)

      robin: Can I ask what you're doing there?

      choice
        Give her a short answer about your condition.
          logDecision("kap4_erklaerung_kurz")
          You explain it briefly. It's a little uncomfortable for you.

          lumi: <mood_scham> I keep telling myself this shouldn't feel embarrassing anymore.
          pauseBeat(2)
          Robin notices your discomfort and stops asking questions.
          pauseBeat(2)
          You continue to eat.
          ....munchmunch....
          pauseBeat(3)
          Then you head back, make a coffee and start working again.
          pauseBeat(2)
          You are quite happy since you get to work on Project Penguin, your favourite project so far.

        Give an enthusiastic answer.
          logDecision("kap4_erklaerung_enthusiastisch")
          You're happy to talk about it. 
          pauseBeat(2) 
          She asks some more questions like "Can you eat sugar?" and "Doesn't it hurt?"
          pauseBeat(2)
          You tell her about your favourite cake your neighbourhood bakery makes and that it usually doesn't hurt.
          pauseBeat(2)
          You continue to eat.

          lumi: <mood_stolz> No reason to hide it.
          ....munchmunch....
          pauseBeat(3)
          Then you head back, make a coffee and start working again.
          pauseBeat(2)
          You are quite happy since you get to work on Project Penguin, your favourite project so far.
          

    Inject at your desk, then join them later.
      oeffentlichSpritzen := arbeitsplatz
      logDecision("kap4_arbeitsplatz")
      playSound("pen_open", "Insulin pen opens")
      playSound("inject", "Injection")

      lumi: <mood_scham> Easier this way. No questions, no explaining.
      pauseBeat(2)
      lumi: <mood_scham> I'm trying to work on not feeling this way anymore. I just... can't always help it.
      pauseBeat(2)
      You head over to the cantine, get the daily menu and join Robin at the table.
      pauseBeat(2)
      ....munchmunch....
      pauseBeat(3)
      Then you head back, make a coffee and start working again.
      pauseBeat(2)
      You are quite happy since you get to work on Project Penguin, your favourite project so far.
      

  -> SpritzenWissen

beat SpritzenWissen
  choice
    - ❓ Learn more
        unlockKnowledge("oeffentlich_spritzen", "Injecting in public", "Many people with Type 1 diabetes feel uneasy about injecting insulin in public, especially soon after diagnosis. Some are happy to answer questions. Others prefer to keep that moment to themselves. Both are perfectly fine.")

        <knowledge>
        Some people with Type 1 diabetes feel uneasy about injecting insulin in public, especially soon after diagnosis. Some are happy to answer questions. Others prefer to keep that moment to themselves. Both are perfectly fine.

        pauseBeat(3)

        -> SpritzenWissen

    NEXT
      -> Nachmittag


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// CHAPTER 5 — Afternoon
// ------------------------------------------------------------

beat Nachmittag
  playSound("office_door_close", "Office door closes behind you")

  Back at your desk, your blood glucose is slowly dropping again. It's
  settling in nicely. Finally you can get back to your main project.

  uhrzeit := "15:30"
  $uhrzeit. Hunger is slowly setting in.

  setCurve(4.4, "falling", uhrzeit)

  balu: Hey.

  You ignore him. You're in the flow.

  pauseBeat(2)

  setCurve(4.1, "falling", uhrzeit)

  balu: Hey!

  lumi: <mood_hungrig> I'm getting hungry...

  In a minute, you say.

  pauseBeat(2)

  setCurve(3.8, "falling", uhrzeit)

  balu: HEY! I'm talking to you!

  playSound("glucose_alarm", "Glucose alarm sounds")

  Only now do you look up.

  A small apple. Quickly.

  Annoyed, you eat it. You hadn't been able to concentrate properly
  for a while anyway.

  applyEffect("traubenzucker", 2, 300)

  lumi: <mood_unsicher> Wait... why did that even happen? I didn't do anything different today.

  balu: I don't make the rules here.

  playSound("teams_notification", "Teams message arrives — it's Robin")

  While you're eating, a Teams message comes in from Robin.

  robin: Heard anything from the client yet?

  "No."

  robin: Feel like a spontaneous drink after work? We're heading out at the end of the day.

  You think about it for a moment. Then you smile.

  "Yeah, sure! I'm in."

  -> HypoWissen

beat HypoWissen
  choice
    - ❓ Learn more
        unlockKnowledge("hypo_anzeichen", "How a low announces itself", "A low blood sugar often announces itself quietly: trouble concentrating, shakiness, sweating, or a sudden intense hunger can be early signs. If you're very busy, it's easy to miss these signals.")

        <knowledge>
        A low blood sugar often announces itself quietly: trouble
        concentrating, shakiness, sweating, or a sudden intense hunger
        can be early signs. If you're very busy, it's easy to miss
        these signals.

        pauseBeat(3)

        -> HypoWissen

    NEXT
      -> NachmittagAbschluss


beat NachmittagAbschluss
  The afternoon drags on. Then, just before the end of the workday:

  uhrzeit := "17:45"
  $uhrzeit.

  You close your laptop. End of the workday.

  -> Apero


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// CHAPTER 6 — Drinks
// ------------------------------------------------------------

beat Apero
  playAmbience("restaurant_ambience", true, "Conversations and glasses in the background")

  On the table: chips, cheese, bread, nuts. A glass of prosecco.
  You don't know the exact carb count.

  pauseBeat(1)

  You check your phone.

  setCurve(5.4, "stable", uhrzeit)

  balu: 5.4 mmol/l.

  pauseBeat(1)

  lumi: <mood_unsicher> Should I bolus conservatively, or just go with my normal guess? Will that be fine, Balu?

  pauseBeat(1)

  balu: Probably.

  pauseBeat(1)

  lumi: <mood_frust> What do you mean, "probably"?

  pauseBeat(1)

  balu: I'm no oracle.

  pauseBeat(2)

  What do you do?

  choice
    Bolus conservatively — less than you think you need.
      kohlenhydratSchaetzung := vorsichtig
      logDecision("kap6_vorsichtig")
      applyEffect("kohlenhydrate_mahlzeit", 1.5, 900)

    Bolus the normal amount you guessed.
      kohlenhydratSchaetzung := genau
      logDecision("kap6_genau")
      applyEffect("kohlenhydrate_mahlzeit", 2.5, 900)

  -> SchaetzenWissen

beat SchaetzenWissen
  choice
    - ❓ Learn more
        unlockKnowledge("kohlenhydrate_schaetzen", "Estimating carbohydrates", "Many people with Type 1 diabetes use apps that photograph meals and help estimate carbohydrates. They can help, but they never replace one's own experience.")

        <knowledge>
        Many people with Type 1 diabetes use apps that photograph
        meals and help estimate carbohydrates. They can help, but
        they never replace one's own experience.

        pauseBeat(3)

        -> SchaetzenWissen

    NEXT
      -> AperoWeiter


beat AperoWeiter
  if kohlenhydratSchaetzung == "vorsichtig"
    -> AperoErgebnisA
  else
    -> AperoErgebnisB


beat AperoErgebnisA
  You bolused conservatively. Later, at home, the curve shows a
  clearly raised reading.

  setCurve(12, "rising", uhrzeit)

  "Hm. Maybe I could have given a bit more insulin. It's okay.
  I'll correct now."

  lumi: <mood_zuversichtlich> It's okay. We'll manage this.

  applyEffect("bolus_correction", 1.5, 900)

  -> Zuhause


beat AperoErgebnisB
  You bolused the amount you guessed. Later, at home, the curve
  looks calm.

  setCurve(7, "stable", uhrzeit)

  Not too high, not too low. This time, the guess held up. That
  doesn't mean it always will.

  -> UnvorhersehbarkeitWissen

beat UnvorhersehbarkeitWissen
  choice
    - ❓ Learn more
        unlockKnowledge("unvorhersehbarkeit", "Why nothing can be planned perfectly", "Even with experience, there's no such thing as a perfect glucose curve. Many factors act at once.")

        <knowledge>
        Even with experience, there's no such thing as a perfect
        glucose curve. Many factors act at once.

        pauseBeat(3)

        -> UnvorhersehbarkeitWissen

    NEXT
      -> Zuhause


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// CHAPTER 7 — Home
// ------------------------------------------------------------

beat Zuhause
  What a day.

  playSound("front_door", "Front door shuts as you get home")
  uhrzeit := "19:00"

  playSound("whatsapp_notification", "WhatsApp message arrives")

  chef: The client's thrilled! Detailed proposal next week!

  You smile.

  lumi: <mood_gluecklich> Made it.

  playSound("fridge_open", "Fridge opens")

  It's been a long day. You open the fridge. Tonight you're in the mood for...

  choice
    Cooking something balanced
      abendessenWahl := kochen
      playSound("cooking_pan", "Pan on the stove")
      applyEffect("kohlenhydrate_mahlzeit", 2, 1200)

      Roasted vegetables with baked feta. A small chocolate mousse
      for dessert on the side.

    Something quick
      abendessenWahl := schnelles
      applyEffect("kohlenhydrate_mahlzeit", 2, 900)

      Yesterday's pasta, with a bit more salad and a bit less
      pasta than usual. Maybe that'll keep the rise a little lower.

    Ordering in
      abendessenWahl := bestellen
      playSound("doorbell", "Doorbell rings")
      applyEffect("kohlenhydrate_mahlzeit", 3, 900)

      Tacos.

  playSound("pen_open", "Insulin pen opens")
  playSound("inject", "Injection")
  Pinch.

  "Ow." Just for a moment. Then the feeling fades again.

  insulinOnBoard += 2

  -> SchmerzWissen

beat SchmerzWissen
  choice
    - ❓ Learn more
        unlockKnowledge("spritzen_schmerz", "Does injecting hurt?", "Injecting insulin is usually barely painful. Sometimes the needle prick can briefly sting. Different countries use different systems, such as pens, pumps, or single-use syringes. The same therapies aren't available everywhere in the world.")

        <knowledge>
        Injecting insulin is usually barely painful. Sometimes the
        needle prick can briefly sting. Different countries use
        different systems, such as pens, pumps, or single-use
        syringes. The same therapies aren't available everywhere in
        the world.

        pauseBeat(3)

        -> SchmerzWissen

    NEXT
      -> DecisionFatigueWissen


beat DecisionFatigueWissen
  choice
    - ❓ Learn more
        unlockKnowledge("decision_fatigue", "Decision fatigue", "People with Type 1 diabetes make dozens of health-related decisions every day. With each additional decision, so-called decision fatigue can build up. Not every decision feels as easy in the evening as it does in the morning.")

        <knowledge>
        People with Type 1 diabetes make dozens of health-related
        decisions every day. With each additional decision, so-called
        decision fatigue can build up. Not every decision feels as
        easy in the evening as it does in the morning.

        pauseBeat(3)

        -> DecisionFatigueWissen

    NEXT
      -> Spaziergang


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// CHAPTER 8 — Walk & Closing
// ------------------------------------------------------------

beat Spaziergang
  After dinner you check your phone once more. The reading is a bit
  higher than expected.

  You put your shoes on. The front door clicks shut.

  playSound("front_door", "Front door shuts")
  playAmbience("evening_walk", true, "Walking along a forest path in the evening")

  All you hear are your footsteps.

  playSound("footsteps_calm", "Calm footsteps")

  bewegungAktiv = true
  applyEffect("bewegung_spaziergang", 1.5, 900)

  Slowly, the curve begins to fall.

  lumi: <mood_entspannt> This feels good right now.

  -> SpaziergangWissen

beat SpaziergangWissen
  choice
    - ❓ Learn more
        unlockKnowledge("spaziergang_wirkung", "Movement after eating", "Even a short walk after a meal can help flatten a glucose rise. How strong the effect is varies from person to person.")

        <knowledge>
        Even a short walk after a meal can help flatten a glucose
        rise. How strong the effect is varies from person to person.

        pauseBeat(3)

        -> SpaziergangWissen

    NEXT
      -> SpaziergangAbschluss


beat SpaziergangAbschluss
  This morning's stress crosses your mind again.

  That was tiring, alright.

  But if you're honest: the day was actually pretty okay.

  -> Abschluss


beat Abschluss
  Your steps carry on. The screen grows quieter.
  The glucose curve settles.

  And then there was that thing with the client. The boss got in touch.
  Handled. A small footnote to the day, nothing more.

  playAmbience("evening_walk", false, "Forest sounds fade as the walk ends")

  What surprised you most today?

  Which decision was hardest for you?

  What will you take away from today?

  pauseBeat(4)

  if persona == "keinDiabetes"
    One last question before you scroll on.

    How often today did you think about your own blood glucose?

    pauseBeat(3)

    Probably never. That's perfectly normal, when a body just
    regulates it by itself.

    While playing this role, you made roughly 25 to 40 decisions
    that were directly or indirectly related to diabetes.

    For people with Type 1 diabetes, that's not a game. That's today.
    And tomorrow again.

    pauseBeat(3)

  Today you experienced a day with Type 1 diabetes.

  Not perfect. Not extraordinary. Just a completely ordinary day.

  Many of these decisions stay invisible to outsiders.
  Yet they accompany every single day.

  pauseBeat(3)

  Remember the question from the start?

  if anfangsEinschaetzung == "zuversichtlich"
    At the start, you felt confident about it.
  else if anfangsEinschaetzung == "unsicher"
    At the start, you weren't quite sure what to make of it.
  else
    At the start, you thought it sounded complicated.

  How do you see it now?

  choice
    I think it went pretty well.
      -> Kurvenaufloesung

    I think it didn't go so well.
      -> Kurvenaufloesung

    No idea.
      -> Kurvenaufloesung


beat Kurvenaufloesung
  pauseBeat(2)

  revealDayCurve()

  Here it is. Your glucose curve from today, from 4am until now.

  pauseBeat(4)

  There is no good. There is no bad.

  There is only: what the body is doing right now, and what you're doing right now.

  pauseBeat(3)

  -> QuizAngebot


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// CHAPTER 8 (cont.) — Optional Quiz
// Ungraded, self-check only. One question per knowledge bite from
// the day. Entirely skippable — opting out goes straight to the
// closing beat below, no penalty, no comment either way.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

beat QuizAngebot
  Before we close, want to check what stuck? A few quick questions,
  nothing scored, nothing graded.

  choice
    Sure, let's see what stuck.
      -> QuizFrage1

    No thanks, straight to the end for me.
      -> AbschlussDank


beat QuizFrage1
  What happens to insulin production in Type 1 diabetes?

  choice
    It slows down gradually over many years.
      -> QuizFrage1Antwort

    It stops almost completely, so insulin has to come from outside.
      -> QuizFrage1Antwort

    It increases at first, then normalises.
      -> QuizFrage1Antwort

beat QuizFrage1Antwort
  In Type 1, the pancreas stops producing insulin almost entirely —
  that's why it has to be supplied from outside, several times a day.

  pauseBeat(2)
  -> QuizFrage2


beat QuizFrage2
  What's the difference between basal and bolus insulin?

  choice
    Basal covers the baseline need over many hours, bolus covers meals and corrections.
      -> QuizFrage2Antwort

    They're two names for the same thing.
      -> QuizFrage2Antwort

    Bolus is for the baseline, basal is for meals.
      -> QuizFrage2Antwort

beat QuizFrage2Antwort
  Basal insulin acts slowly over many hours and covers the body's
  baseline need. Bolus insulin is for meals or for correcting a high
  reading.

  pauseBeat(2)
  -> QuizFrage3


beat QuizFrage3
  Why can mornings need more insulin than other times of day?

  choice
    People simply eat more at breakfast.
      -> QuizFrage3Antwort

    Hormones released just before waking make many people temporarily more insulin-resistant.
      -> QuizFrage3Antwort

    Insulin doesn't work at all in the morning.
      -> QuizFrage3Antwort

beat QuizFrage3Antwort
  Hormones released just before waking make many people temporarily
  more insulin-resistant — the same breakfast can play out completely
  differently on different days.

  pauseBeat(2)
  -> QuizFrage4


beat QuizFrage4
  Besides carbohydrates, what else affects the glucose curve?

  choice
    Nothing else really matters.
      -> QuizFrage4Antwort

    Protein and fat too, usually more slowly.
      -> QuizFrage4Antwort

    Only how much water you drink with the meal.
      -> QuizFrage4Antwort

beat QuizFrage4Antwort
  Protein and fat influence the curve too — usually more slowly and
  over a longer period than carbohydrates alone.

  pauseBeat(2)
  -> QuizFrage5


beat QuizFrage5
  How does physical activity affect blood glucose?

  choice
    It always raises it.
      -> QuizFrage5Antwort

    It always lowers it.
      -> QuizFrage5Antwort

    Depending on the situation, it can cause a quick rise or a quick drop.
      -> QuizFrage5Antwort

beat QuizFrage5Antwort
  Depending on the situation, activity can push the curve either way —
  a quick rise or a quick drop, sometimes unpredictably.

  pauseBeat(2)
  -> QuizFrage6


beat QuizFrage6
  Why can stress raise blood glucose?

  choice
    Hormones like cortisol and adrenaline cause the liver to release glucose.
      -> QuizFrage6Antwort

    Stress has no real effect on glucose.
      -> QuizFrage6Antwort

    Stress only affects mood, never the body.
      -> QuizFrage6Antwort

beat QuizFrage6Antwort
  Stress hormones like cortisol and adrenaline cause the liver to
  release glucose — a normal, involuntary response of the body.

  pauseBeat(2)
  -> QuizFrage7


beat QuizFrage7
  How do people with Type 1 diabetes generally feel about injecting in public?

  choice
    Everyone feels exactly the same way about it.
      -> QuizFrage7Antwort

    It varies — some are comfortable, some prefer privacy, both are fine.
      -> QuizFrage7Antwort

    It's generally discouraged by doctors.
      -> QuizFrage7Antwort

beat QuizFrage7Antwort
  It varies from person to person. Some are happy to answer questions,
  others prefer to keep that moment to themselves — both are perfectly
  fine.

  pauseBeat(2)
  -> QuizFrage8


beat QuizFrage8
  What are early signs that a low blood sugar might be announcing itself?

  choice
    A rash on the skin.
      -> QuizFrage8Antwort

    Trouble concentrating, shakiness, sweating, sudden hunger.
      -> QuizFrage8Antwort

    There usually are no warning signs at all.
      -> QuizFrage8Antwort

beat QuizFrage8Antwort
  Trouble concentrating, shakiness, sweating, or a sudden intense
  hunger can all be early signs — easy to miss when you're busy.

  pauseBeat(2)
  -> QuizFrage9


beat QuizFrage9
  What can help with estimating carbohydrates in a meal?

  choice
    Apps that photograph meals can help, but don't replace experience.
      -> QuizFrage9Antwort

    There's no way to estimate them at all.
      -> QuizFrage9Antwort

    Restaurants always list the exact carb count.
      -> QuizFrage9Antwort

beat QuizFrage9Antwort
  Apps that photograph meals and estimate carbohydrates can help — but
  they never fully replace one's own experience.

  pauseBeat(2)
  -> QuizFrage10


beat QuizFrage10
  Even with a lot of experience, is a perfectly predictable glucose curve possible?

  choice
    Yes, always, if you do everything right.
      -> QuizFrage10Antwort

    No — many factors act at once, so it's never fully predictable.
      -> QuizFrage10Antwort

    Only for people who don't take insulin.
      -> QuizFrage10Antwort

beat QuizFrage10Antwort
  No — even with experience, there's no such thing as a perfect
  glucose curve. Many factors act at once.

  pauseBeat(2)
  -> QuizFrage11


beat QuizFrage11
  Does injecting insulin usually hurt?

  choice
    It's usually barely painful, sometimes a brief sting.
      -> QuizFrage11Antwort

    It's always very painful.
      -> QuizFrage11Antwort

    It's painless — no needle is involved.
      -> QuizFrage11Antwort

beat QuizFrage11Antwort
  Injecting is usually barely painful — sometimes just a brief sting.
  Different countries use different systems: pens, pumps, or
  single-use syringes.

  pauseBeat(2)
  -> QuizFrage12


beat QuizFrage12
  What is decision fatigue, in the context of Type 1 diabetes?

  choice
    A medical complication caused by high blood sugar.
      -> QuizFrage12Antwort

    The build-up from making dozens of health-related decisions every day.
      -> QuizFrage12Antwort

    A sleep disorder unrelated to diabetes.
      -> QuizFrage12Antwort

beat QuizFrage12Antwort
  People with Type 1 diabetes make dozens of health-related decisions
  every day. Each additional one can add to decision fatigue — not
  every decision feels as easy in the evening as in the morning.

  pauseBeat(2)
  -> QuizFrage13


beat QuizFrage13
  How can a short walk after a meal affect a glucose rise?

  choice
    It has no effect at all.
      -> QuizFrage13Antwort

    It can help flatten the rise, though the effect varies from person to person.
      -> QuizFrage13Antwort

    It always causes a low.
      -> QuizFrage13Antwort

beat QuizFrage13Antwort
  Even a short walk after eating can help flatten a glucose rise —
  how strong the effect is varies from person to person.

  pauseBeat(2)

  That's everything from today.

  -> AbschlussDank


beat AbschlussDank
  if playerName != ""
    Thank you for going through today with him.
  else
    Thank you for going through today with him.

  What are you thinking now?

  offerShare()

  -> .
